# Exercise: Measure Latency
#
# Little's Law: L = λW
# Before we can apply the law, we need to measure W (latency).
#
# W = the average time an item spends in the system.
# Here, we'll time a single item through a worker:
#   send item → worker processes (sleeps 10ms) → receive result
#
# time.now returns milliseconds. Measure elapsed time as:
#   time.now ... time.now swap i.-
#
# Your task: Receive the result and calculate elapsed time.
# The elapsed time should be >= 10ms.
# When done, delete the marker line below.

# I AM NOT DONE

: test-measure-latency ( -- )
    chan.make   # work channel
    chan.make   # results channel

    # Spawn worker: receive item, sleep 10ms, send to results
    2dup [ over chan.receive drop 10 time.sleep-ms swap chan.send drop drop ] strand.spawn drop
    drop drop

    # Record start time
    time.now

    # Send work item
    rot 1 swap chan.send drop

    # Your code here:
    # 1. Receive result from results channel (second channel on stack)
    # 2. Drop the received value
    # 3. Record end time with time.now
    # 4. Calculate elapsed = end - start
    # 5. Clean up stack to leave just elapsed time
    # Hint: the stack has ( results start ) at this point
    drop drop 0

    # Do not edit below this line
    # Elapsed time should be at least 10ms
    10 >= test.assert
;
